exports[`planRoutingPullRequest > claims no saving on a public repository and says fork pull requests stay on GitHub-hosted 1`] = `
"<!-- buildhound-route: test,ios -->
This pull request runs 2 jobs on the machines you connected to Buildhound, with GitHub-hosted runners as the fallback. Only \`runs-on\` changes; comments and formatting are kept.

| Workflow | Job | Today | After this pull request |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`ci.yml\` | \`test\` | \`ubuntu-latest\` | \`\${{ ((github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository) \\|\\| github.event_name == 'push' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'schedule' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'merge_group' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'release') && vars.BUILDHOUND_LINUX \\|\\| 'ubuntu-latest' }}\` |
| \`ci.yml\` | \`ios\` | \`macos-15\` | \`\${{ ((github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository) \\|\\| github.event_name == 'push' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'schedule' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'merge_group' \\|\\| github.event_name == 'release') && vars.BUILDHOUND_MACOS \\|\\| 'macos-15' }}\` |

### Speed

| Job | GitHub-hosted | Your machines | Speed-up |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`test\` | 5 min, median of 40 runs | 2 min 30 s, median of 5 runs | 2.00x faster |
| \`ios\` | no successful runs | not run there yet | not measured yet |

Medians of successful runs in the last 30 days. A speed-up is shown only once a job has at least 3 successful runs on each side; until then it is not measured yet and none is claimed. Run \`npx buildhound route --apply\` again later to update this table.

### Monthly saving

This repository is public, so GitHub-hosted runners cost it nothing and no saving is claimed. It routes for speed, on any plan.

### Every option, ranked by computed cost

| Job | Stay on GitHub-hosted | Cheapest by computed cost |
|---|---|---|
| \`test\` | $10.08 a month | \`jog\`, $2.02 a month |
| \`ios\` | $55.80 a month | \`buildhound-macos\` (buildhound), $24.00 a month |

From \`npx buildhound route\`, which prices every candidate from published rates at today's minutes, with your machines at the Team fee per machine. Buildhound has no referral agreement with any runner vendor.

### To stay on GitHub-hosted

Close this pull request. Nothing changes, and running \`npx buildhound route --apply\` again will not open it again.

### When it takes effect

Merging changes nothing by itself. Each routed job reads the repository variable \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX\` and \`BUILDHOUND_MACOS\` and runs on its GitHub-hosted label while the variable is empty. Buildhound's control plane sets it once a connected machine serves this repository, and clears it when none is online, so new jobs route to the healthy side.

On this public repository the expression sends a job to your machines only for push, schedule, workflow_dispatch, merge_group, release and pull requests from branches of this repository. Fork pull requests and events anyone on GitHub can cause stay on GitHub-hosted.

### Not routed

- \`ci.yml\`, job \`deploy\` stays on \`ubuntu-latest\`: it can reach secrets or write to the repository, so only a machine a person marked Trusted may run it, and Buildhound does not route it (jobs.deploy.environment: production).

### Assumption

This changes where the jobs run. Confirm before merging:
- each job runs the same on your machine as on GitHub-hosted. Buildhound's images mirror GitHub's runners, but a tool only GitHub's image has, or a service only reachable from GitHub's network, would make a job fail there.
- no Linux job needs an x86-64 kernel. On a Mac, \`buildhound-linux\` is an arm64 Ubuntu 24.04 VM that runs x86-64 binaries and Docker images through Rosetta; a job that needs x86-64 natively reads \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX_X64\` instead.

### Change

- \`.github/workflows/ci.yml\`: 2 lines added, 2 removed.

<details><summary>Diff</summary>

\`\`\`diff
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -5,11 +5,11 @@
 jobs:
   # Lint and unit tests
   test:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    runs-on: \${{ ((github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository) || github.event_name == 'push' || github.event_name == 'schedule' || github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' || github.event_name == 'merge_group' || github.event_name == 'release') && vars.BUILDHOUND_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}
     steps:
       - run: npm test
   ios:
-    runs-on: macos-15 # Xcode 16
+    runs-on: \${{ ((github.event_name == 'pull_request' && github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository) || github.event_name == 'push' || github.event_name == 'schedule' || github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' || github.event_name == 'merge_group' || github.event_name == 'release') && vars.BUILDHOUND_MACOS || 'macos-15' }} # Xcode 16
     steps:
       - run: xcodebuild test
   deploy:
\`\`\`

</details>

---

Opened by [Buildhound](https://buildhound.sh) with \`npx buildhound route --apply\`. Review it like any other change; running the command again updates this pull request instead of opening another."
`;

exports[`planRoutingPullRequest > routes a secret-bearing job to the trusted variable and says which jobs go to Trusted machines and why (CLAUDE.md rule 19) 1`] = `
"<!-- buildhound-route: test,ios,deploy -->
This pull request runs 3 jobs on the machines you connected to Buildhound, with GitHub-hosted runners as the fallback. Only \`runs-on\` changes; comments and formatting are kept.

| Workflow | Job | Today | After this pull request |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`ci.yml\` | \`test\` | \`ubuntu-latest\` | \`\${{ vars.BUILDHOUND_LINUX \\|\\| 'ubuntu-latest' }}\` |
| \`ci.yml\` | \`ios\` | \`macos-15\` | \`\${{ vars.BUILDHOUND_MACOS \\|\\| 'macos-15' }}\` |
| \`ci.yml\` | \`deploy\` | \`ubuntu-latest\` | \`\${{ vars.BUILDHOUND_LINUX_TRUSTED \\|\\| 'ubuntu-latest' }}\` |

### Jobs that use secrets

This job reads \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX_TRUSTED\`, which names only machines a person marked Trusted, because it can reach secrets or write to the repository:

- \`ci.yml\`, job \`deploy\`: jobs.deploy.environment: production.

Until a Trusted machine is connected and serves this repository, \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX_TRUSTED\` stays unset and this job runs on GitHub-hosted, as today. A machine marked No secrets never runs it, since Buildhound checks every job before a machine takes it.

### Speed

| Job | GitHub-hosted | Your machines | Speed-up |
|---|---|---|---|
| \`test\` | 5 min, median of 40 runs | 2 min 30 s, median of 5 runs | 2.00x faster |
| \`ios\` | no successful runs | not run there yet | not measured yet |
| \`deploy\` | no successful runs | not run there yet | not measured yet |

Medians of successful runs in the last 30 days. A speed-up is shown only once a job has at least 3 successful runs on each side; until then it is not measured yet and none is claimed. Run \`npx buildhound route --apply\` again later to update this table.

### Monthly saving

\`\`\`
GitHub-hosted for these jobs   $66.60 a month, 2700 minutes
Team, 1 machine at $24.00      -$24.00 a month
Saving                         $42.60 a month
\`\`\`

About $42.60 a month saved on machines you already have, after Team at $24.00 a machine a month (the monthly price; nothing to buy).

GitHub-hosted is priced from the last 30 days of runs, scaled to 30 days, at GitHub's list price. On your machines a job costs nothing a minute; the plan is a flat fee per machine on the days it runs a job. 1 machine for the 1 macOS job seen at once, two macOS jobs at once per Mac; Linux jobs share the same machines.

### Every option, ranked by computed cost

| Job | Stay on GitHub-hosted | Cheapest by computed cost |
|---|---|---|
| \`test\` | $10.08 a month | \`jog\`, $2.02 a month |
| \`ios\` | $55.80 a month | \`buildhound-macos\` (buildhound), $24.00 a month |

From \`npx buildhound route\`, which prices every candidate from published rates at today's minutes, with your machines at the Team fee per machine. Buildhound has no referral agreement with any runner vendor.

### To stay on GitHub-hosted

Close this pull request. Nothing changes, and running \`npx buildhound route --apply\` again will not open it again.

### When it takes effect

Merging changes nothing by itself. Each routed job reads the repository variable \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX\` and \`BUILDHOUND_MACOS\` and runs on its GitHub-hosted label while the variable is empty. Buildhound's control plane sets it once a connected machine serves this repository and the account has Personal or Team, and clears it when none is online, so new jobs route to the healthy side.

The jobs that use secrets read \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX_TRUSTED\` and run on their GitHub-hosted label while it is empty. Buildhound's control plane sets it only once a machine a person marked Trusted serves this repository and the account has Personal or Team, and clears it when none can take their jobs, so new jobs route to the healthy side.

### Assumption

This changes where the jobs run. Confirm before merging:
- each job runs the same on your machine as on GitHub-hosted. Buildhound's images mirror GitHub's runners, but a tool only GitHub's image has, or a service only reachable from GitHub's network, would make a job fail there.
- no Linux job needs an x86-64 kernel. On a Mac, \`buildhound-linux\` is an arm64 Ubuntu 24.04 VM that runs x86-64 binaries and Docker images through Rosetta; a job that needs x86-64 natively reads \`BUILDHOUND_LINUX_X64\` instead.
- the saving uses today's GitHub-hosted minutes and assumes each job takes as long on your machine, since vendor claims are never used.
- the saving counts the jobs that use secrets, which run on your machines only once a person marks one Trusted.

### Change

- \`.github/workflows/ci.yml\`: 3 lines added, 3 removed.

<details><summary>Diff</summary>

\`\`\`diff
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -5,15 +5,15 @@
 jobs:
   # Lint and unit tests
   test:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    runs-on: \${{ vars.BUILDHOUND_LINUX || 'ubuntu-latest' }}
     steps:
       - run: npm test
   ios:
-    runs-on: macos-15 # Xcode 16
+    runs-on: \${{ vars.BUILDHOUND_MACOS || 'macos-15' }} # Xcode 16
     steps:
       - run: xcodebuild test
   deploy:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    runs-on: \${{ vars.BUILDHOUND_LINUX_TRUSTED || 'ubuntu-latest' }}
     environment: production
     steps:
       - run: ./deploy
\`\`\`

</details>

---

Opened by [Buildhound](https://buildhound.sh) with \`npx buildhound route --apply\`. Review it like any other change; running the command again updates this pull request instead of opening another."
`;

